<%# THE APPLICATIONS SUMMARY CARD — the first of the four /deployments summary cards
    (tasks/_deploy_summary_row) — plus the pinned strip that follows it down the page.

    ONE LINE PER APP, NEWEST SUITE RESTART FIRST. The operator's spec: "whichever app
    test suite restarted most recently jumps to the top", so a glance at the top row
    answers "what is cooking and how is it going". The order is
    Ci::AppLadder.recent_first, applied once in the controller and the broadcaster, so
    this card, the sidebar's full cards and the pinned strip all read in ONE order.

    EACH LINE IS THE SAME METER as everywhere else on the page —
    components/_ci_progress_meter, inline — so the marks keep their meaning: ✗ failures
    pushed left, a spinner while a check runs, ✓ passes pushed right, and a clock that
    ticks while the suite runs and freezes to its duration once it settles.

    THE FULL CARDS LIVE IN THE SIDEBAR (tasks/_app_ladder_detail, #app-ladder-detail).
    A click anywhere on this card opens them; nothing on the card is a link of its own,
    because a row that navigated to GitHub would make "click to see more" a coin toss.

    #app-ladder-row IS STILL THE BROADCAST SLOT, and that is load-bearing rather than
    cosmetic. The board takes live updates over Turbo Streams, and a stream can only
    replace a target it can name — DeploymentsBroadcaster.app_ladder replaces THIS id
    (and #app-ladder-detail beside it) on every CI tick for a ladder branch, every rung
    stamp and every release event. Without it the card sat stale until a reload while
    every other card moved, which is how the slot came to exist.

    THE PINNED STRIP is rendered HERE, inside #app-ladder-row, on purpose: the broadcast
    replaces this slot, so the strip refreshes with the card and can never sit pinned to
    the top of the page showing a verdict the card has already moved past. It pins once
    this card has scrolled above the site header, and carries the full condensed tiles
    (name, meter, ladder) the operator reads while working the board below.

    data-rendered-at is the slot's own render stamp — "when was this last refreshed" for
    anyone who suspects a stale board, and what makes the wiring e2e deterministic: it
    changes on every render, so it proves a broadcast replaced the slot whether or not
    the content happened to differ.

    Locals: cards - Array<Ci::AppLadder::Card>, in display order. %>
<% cards = local_assigns.fetch(:cards, []) %>
<%# The pinned strip's fade — seeded server-side from the card count so its first paint
    is right before Alpine measures anything (Ci::AppLadder::ROW_FADE_AT is the count
    that cannot fit the board's widest container). %>
<% seed_fade = cards.size >= Ci::AppLadder::ROW_FADE_AT %>
<% running = cards.count { |card| card.latest_suite_progress&.running? } %>
<div id="app-ladder-row" class="min-w-0" data-test="app-ladder-row"
     data-rendered-at="<%= (Time.current.to_f * 1000).to_i %>">
  <%# THE CONTROLLER, inline. No <script> tag: this fragment is replaced by a Turbo
      Stream broadcast, and Alpine re-initialises replaced DOM on its own while a
      script inside a replaced fragment is a re-execution question nobody should have
      to answer on a live board.

      It decides ONE thing — whether the card has scrolled above the pinned stack, so
      the strip should show — and gauges the strip's own fade. The answer is written to
      the appLadder STORE, which outlives this node (see tasks/_deploy_board): a fresh
      component starting at false is what made the strip blink out and back on every
      broadcast.

      The ResizeObserver belongs to the instance and is released in destroy(): this
      slot is REPLACED wholesale by broadcasts all day, so an observer left behind
      would accumulate per broadcast. %>
  <div class="h-full"
       x-data="{
         fadeRight: '<%= ApplicationHelper::APP_LADDER_FADE_MASK %>',
         pinnedOverflowing: <%= seed_fade %>,
         pinnedAtEnd: false,
         _ro: null,
         _dead: false,
         init() {
           this.$nextTick(() => {
             if (this._dead) return;
             this.measure();
             if (!window.ResizeObserver) return;
             this._ro = new ResizeObserver(() => this.measure());
             const header = document.querySelector('.vt-pinned-header');
             if (header) this._ro.observe(header);
             if (this.$refs.pinnedScroller) this._ro.observe(this.$refs.pinnedScroller);
           });
         },
         destroy() {
           this._dead = true;
           if (this._ro) { this._ro.disconnect(); this._ro = null; }
         },
         measure() {
           // READ THE PUBLISHED EDGE, DO NOT MEASURE THE HEADER. The engine
           // publishes the pinned stack from one coalesced measurement; the strip
           // positions off the same variable, so the question asked -- has the
           // card scrolled above where I would pin? -- and the answer acted on
           // cannot drift apart.
           //
           // NO DOUBLE QUOTES ANYWHERE IN THIS ATTRIBUTE. x-data is delimited by
           // them, so one inside -- even inside a comment -- ends the attribute
           // early and Alpine never parses the component.
           // test/views/app_ladder_row_test.rb guards this.
           const edge = parseFloat(
             getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue('--pin-apps-top')
           ) || 0;
           const row = this.$refs.row;
           // A card with no box (a hidden parent, a detached node) is not a card
           // that scrolled away: an all-zero rect would otherwise read as pinned.
           const box = row ? row.getBoundingClientRect() : null;
           this.$store.appLadder.pinned = !!box && box.height > 0 && box.bottom <= edge;
           this.gauge();
         },
         gauge() {
           const strip = this.$refs.pinnedScroller;
           if (strip) {
             this.pinnedOverflowing = strip.scrollWidth - strip.clientWidth > 8;
             this.pinnedAtEnd = strip.scrollLeft + strip.clientWidth >= strip.scrollWidth - 8;
           }
         }
       }"
       @scroll.window="measure()"
       @resize.window="measure()">
    <%= tag.div(**deploy_summary_card_options("apps", test: "app-summary-card", extra_class: "@container"), "x-ref": "row",
                "data-running": running) do %>
      <%= render "tasks/summary_card_header", panel: "apps", title: "Applications",
                 meta: cards.any? ? (running.positive? ? "#{running} cooking" : pluralize(cards.size, "app")) : nil %>
      <% if cards.any? %>
        <ol class="mt-3 space-y-1.5" data-test="app-summary-list">
          <% cards.each do |card| %>
            <%= render "tasks/app_summary_row", card: card %>
          <% end %>
        </ol>
      <% else %>
        <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-muted" data-test="app-summary-empty">No applications report CI on the ladder yet.</p>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>

    <% if cards.any? %>
      <%# THE PINNED STRIP. Fixed under the site header (z-40 — the header is z-50 and
          must always win), full-bleed so it reads as page chrome, and carrying the same
          container gutters as the board so the tiles line up with the cards they came
          from. `style="display: none"` is the no-JS/pre-init state: nothing is pinned
          until something has measured that the row has actually left the screen. %>
      <%# `:style` BINDS AN OBJECT, and that is a correctness rule rather than a taste
          one. Alpine's STRING form of :style calls setAttribute("style", …), which
          replaces the whole attribute — including the `display: none` that x-show wrote
          — and `offset` changes on every scroll while the header shrinks. So the string
          form would unhide this strip over the top of the board the moment the operator
          scrolled with the row still on screen. The object form sets one property and
          leaves x-show's alone. %>
      <div class="fixed inset-x-0 z-40 border-b border-subtle bg-page/95 shadow-lg supports-[backdrop-filter]:backdrop-blur"
           data-test="app-ladder-pinned"
           role="region"
           aria-label="Applications, pinned to the top of the page"
           x-show="$store.appLadder.pinned"
           <%# top comes from the engine's pinned stack, not from JS. The old
               :style wrote a MEASURED number that chased the site header through
               every intermediate height of its collapse — see the task
               stop-headers-chasing-navbar for the 17 values it stepped through.
               data-pin makes this strip publish its own --pin-apps-* in turn,
               which is what the board's lane headers stack onto.

               --pin-apps-TOP, not --pin-nav-bottom: this strip is ITSELF a layer
               of the stack, and -top is the bottom edge of everything ABOVE it.
               Today that is the site header and the two are equal, so the change
               moves nothing — but naming the nav here is a claim that the nav is
               what sits above this strip, which stops being true the moment
               anything is pinned between them. (-bottom would be worse still: it
               is this strip's OWN edge, so the strip would chase itself down the
               page on every publish.) %>
           data-pin="apps"
           style="display: none; top: var(--pin-apps-top, 0px)"
           x-transition.opacity.duration.150ms>
        <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-screen-2xl px-3 py-1.5 sm:px-4 lg:px-6 xl:px-8">
            <div class="flex min-w-0 items-stretch gap-2 overflow-x-auto"
                 data-test="app-ladder-pinned-scroller"
                 x-ref="pinnedScroller"
                 <%= tag.attributes(data: { faded: true }, style: ApplicationHelper::APP_LADDER_FADE_MASK) if seed_fade %>
                 :data-faded="pinnedOverflowing && !pinnedAtEnd"
                 :style="(pinnedOverflowing && !pinnedAtEnd) ? fadeRight : ''"
                 @scroll="gauge()">
              <% cards.each do |card| %>
                <%= render "tasks/app_ladder_pinned_card", card: card %>
              <% end %>
            </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</div>
